@# Random event sequences available after each English class segment.

@entry school.english.event.group-discussion
  @scene school.english.event.group-discussion
  @pool school.class.english
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "english"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.english.event.reading-aloud
  @scene school.english.event.reading-aloud
  @pool school.class.english
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "english"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@entry school.english.event.surprise-quiz
  @scene school.english.event.surprise-quiz
  @pool school.class.english
  @place-key high_school
  @when school.phase == "class"
  @when school.subjectId == "english"
  @priority 0
  @weight 1
@endentry

@sequence school.english.event.group-discussion -> @return
@heading "An Unexpected Discussion"
@choices "How do you participate?"

@passage opening

The teacher closes the textbook and asks the class to discuss whether a narrator can be trustworthy while still being mistaken.

@if npc.taylor.present
Taylor turns slightly in your direction, clearly waiting to hear what you think.
@else
The room stays awkwardly quiet while everyone avoids being the first to answer.
@endif

@choice discussion-argue "Offer your own interpretation"
  @icon 💡
  @check grade english difficult

  @success -> .argued
    @effect grade english 1
  @endsuccess

  @failure -> .misread
    @effect grade english -1
  @endfailure
@endchoice

@choice discussion-listen "Listen to the other answers" -> .listened
  @icon 👂
@endchoice

@choice discussion-taylor "Compare ideas with Taylor" -> .taylor
  @icon 💬
  @when npc.taylor.present
  @effect relationship taylor 0.005
@endchoice

@passage argued

You explain that a narrator can report events honestly and still misunderstand what those events mean. The teacher nods and uses your answer to move the discussion forward.

@next "Return to the lesson" -> @return

@passage misread

You start confidently, but mix up the narrator's assumptions with the author's intent. The teacher corrects the distinction before moving the discussion on.

@next "Return to the lesson" -> @return

@passage listened

You keep quiet and follow the argument as it moves around the room. A few of the answers are surprisingly convincing.

@next "Return to the lesson" -> @return

@passage taylor

You and Taylor quietly compare interpretations before sharing a combined answer with the class.

@next "Return to the lesson" -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.english.event.reading-aloud -> @return
@heading "Reading Aloud"
@choices "What do you do?"

@passage opening

The teacher asks for someone to read the next page aloud.

@if player.education.english.grade >= 70
The passage is dense, but you recognise the rhythm of the older prose.
@else
The unfamiliar phrasing makes the passage look more intimidating than it sounded a moment ago.
@endif

@choice reading-volunteer "Volunteer to read"
  @icon 📖
  @check skill resolve tricky

  @success -> .steady
    @effect grade english 1
  @endsuccess

  @failure -> .stumbled
    @effect grade english -1
  @endfailure
@endchoice

@choice reading-follow "Follow along quietly" -> .followed
  @icon 👀
@endchoice

@passage steady

You keep a measured pace and land the difficult sentences cleanly. By the end, several classmates are following your rhythm instead of the page.

@next "Return to the lesson" -> @return

@passage stumbled

A sentence catches you halfway through. You recover, but not before the room hears the hesitation.

@next "Return to the lesson" -> @return

@passage followed

Someone else volunteers. You follow the page and make a note beside the phrases the teacher emphasises.

@next "Return to the lesson" -> @return
@endsequence

@sequence school.english.event.surprise-quiz -> @return
@heading "A Surprise Quiz"
@choices "Choose an answer"

@passage first-question

Without warning, the teacher hands out a short quiz. The first question asks which sentence contains a metaphor.

@choice quiz-first-correct "The moon was a silver coin above the rooftops." -> .second-question
  @icon ✍️
  @change grade english 1
@endchoice

@choice quiz-first-wrong "The moon shone as brightly as a lamp." -> .second-question
  @icon ✍️
  @change grade english -1
@endchoice

@choice quiz-first-other "The moon appeared after sunset." -> .second-question
  @icon ✍️
  @change grade english -1
@endchoice

@passage second-question

The second question asks which version uses the apostrophe correctly.

@choice quiz-second-correct "The students' books covered the table." -> .results
  @icon ✍️
  @change grade english 1
@endchoice

@choice quiz-second-wrong-one "The student's books covered the table." -> .results
  @icon ✍️
  @change grade english -1
@endchoice

@choice quiz-second-wrong-two "The students books' covered the table." -> .results
  @icon ✍️
  @change grade english -1
@endchoice

@passage results

The teacher collects the papers almost as quickly as they were handed out.

@if player.education.english.grade >= 70
You feel reasonably confident about what you wrote.
@else
You are left replaying both questions in your head.
@endif

@next "Return to the lesson" -> @return
@endsequence
